    "anthony-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Lisbon, Portugal, and Padua, Italy": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Canonized 1232 by Pope Gregory IX; Doctor of the Church": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "June 13": 1,
      "Fernando Martins was born in Lisbon around 1195 and became an Augustinian canon. When the bodies of five Franciscan martyrs from Morocco passed through Coimbra in 1220, he joined the Franciscans and took the name Anthony. He sailed for Morocco, fell ill and ended up in Italy.": 1,
      "For a time he worked in obscurity, until an unplanned sermon at an ordination showed how gifted a preacher he was. Saint Francis made him the first theology teacher of the Franciscans. He preached across France and northern Italy and died near Padua on June 13, 1231, at about 35. He was canonized in 1232 and named a Doctor of the Church in 1946. People ask for his help with lost things, which is a devotion and not a Church title.": 1,
      "Read the full life of Saint Anthony": 1,
      "→": 1,
      "Sources:": 1,
      "Benedict XVI: Saint Anthony of Padua, February 10, 2010": 1,
      ".": 1,
      "About Saint Anthony of Padua": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "c. 1195-1231": 1
    },
    "charbel-story-v2.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Bekaa Kafra and Annaya, Lebanon": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Canonized October 9, 1977, by Pope Paul VI": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "July 24 (Roman calendar); third Sunday of July (Maronite calendar)": 1,
      "Youssef Makhlouf was a farmer's son from the mountain village of Bekaa Kafra in North Lebanon. His father died when he was three. At 23 he left home to become a monk of the Lebanese Maronite Order and took the name Charbel. He was ordained a priest on July 23, 1859.": 1,
      "For many years he lived in the community at the Monastery of Saint Maron at Annaya. From 1875 he lived as a hermit nearby, in prayer, fasting and manual work. He died on December 24, 1898, eight days after he was taken ill while celebrating Mass. Crowds came to his tomb afterward, Christians and Muslims alike. Pope Paul VI beatified him in 1965 and canonized him in 1977.": 1,
      "Read the full life of Saint Charbel": 1,
      "→": 1,
      "Sources:": 1,
      "Vatican City State: Saint of the day, July 24": 1,
      ".": 1,
      "About Saint Charbel Makhlouf": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "1828-1898": 1
    },
    "francis-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Assisi, Italy": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Canonized 1228 by Pope Gregory IX; patron of ecology": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "October 4": 1,
      "Francis was the son of a rich cloth merchant in Assisi. He wanted to be a knight, but after being taken prisoner in a war and a slow change of heart he gave up his inheritance. He heard Christ speak from the cross at San Damiano and began to live the Gospel in poverty, preaching and joy. Companions joined him, and Pope Innocent III approved his way of life in 1209.": 1,
      "In 1219 he crossed the lines at Damietta to speak with the Sultan, armed only with his faith. In 1223 he set up the first Christmas crib at Greccio. In 1224 he received the stigmata, and near the end of his life, almost blind, he wrote the Canticle of the Creatures. He died on October 3, 1226, and was canonized two years later. Many sayings are wrongly credited to him, including the \"Peace Prayer.\"": 1,
      "Read the full life of Saint Francis": 1,
      "→": 1,
      "Sources:": 1,
      "Benedict XVI: Saint Francis of Assisi, January 27, 2010": 1,
      ".": 1,
      "About Saint Francis of Assisi": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "1181/1182-1226": 1
    },
    "hardini-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Hardin and Kfifan, Lebanon": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Canonized May 16, 2004, by Pope John Paul II": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "December 14 (Maronite calendar)": 1,
      "Youssef Kassab was born in 1808 in the village of Hardin in North Lebanon. In 1828 he entered the Lebanese Maronite Order and took the name Nimatullah, which means \"grace of God\" in Arabic. He was ordained a priest on December 25, 1833.": 1,
      "At the monastery of Kfifan he directed the young monks' studies and taught theology. One of his students was Charbel Makhlouf. He also founded free schools for the children of the mountain villages. Pope John Paul II called him \"a man of prayer, enamored of the Eucharist.\" He died on December 14, 1858.": 1,
      "Read the full life of Saint Nimatullah": 1,
      "→": 1,
      "Holy See: biography of Nimatullah al-Hardini": 1,
      "About Saint Nimatullah al-Hardini": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "1808-1858": 1
    },
    "joseph-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Nazareth and Bethlehem; the flight to Egypt": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Patron of the Universal Church, proclaimed by Pope Pius IX on December 8, 1870": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "March 19": 1,
      "The Gospels of Matthew and Luke tell us what we know about Joseph. He was a carpenter of Nazareth, engaged to Mary. Matthew says he took Mary into his home after an angel spoke to him in a dream. He traveled with her to Bethlehem, where Jesus was born.": 1,
      "When Herod threatened the child, Joseph took the family to Egypt, and later settled in Nazareth. Luke tells of the family's yearly trip to Jerusalem and the day Jesus was found in the Temple. The Gospels do not record a single spoken word from Joseph. Pope Francis wrote in Patris Corde that his strength was in quiet, faithful care. The Church also keeps May 1 as the memorial of Saint Joseph the Worker.": 1,
      "Sources:": 1,
      ";": 2,
      "Matthew 1": 1,
      ".": 1,
      "About Saint Joseph": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "First century": 1
    },
    "jpii-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Wadowice and Krakow, Poland; Rome": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Canonized April 27, 2014, by Pope Francis": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "October 22": 1,
      "Karol Wojtyla was born on May 18, 1920 in Wadowice, Poland. He lost his mother, his brother and his father while still young. During the Nazi occupation he worked in a quarry and a chemical plant and secretly studied for the priesthood. He was ordained on November 1, 1946.": 1,
      "He became Archbishop of Krakow in 1964 and was elected pope on October 16, 1978, the first Pole to hold the office and the first non-Italian since 1523. He visited 129 countries and started World Youth Day. He survived a shooting in Saint Peter's Square on May 13, 1981, and later forgave the gunman. He died on April 2, 2005, and was canonized with Pope John XXIII.": 1,
      "Read the full life of Saint John Paul II": 1,
      "→": 1,
      "Sources:": 1,
      "Holy See: short biography of John Paul II": 1,
      ".": 1,
      "About Saint John Paul II": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "1920-2005": 1
    },
    "jude-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Galilee and Jerusalem": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "One of the Twelve apostles; not Judas Iscariot": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "October 28, with Saint Simon": 1,
      "Jude is one of the Twelve apostles. Matthew and Mark call him Thaddeus, and Luke calls him \"Judas, the son of James.\" Benedict XVI noted that very little about him has come down to us.": 1,
      "The one thing the Gospels record is his question at the Last Supper in John 14:22: \"Lord, how is it that you will manifest yourself to us and not to the world?\" Jesus answers that anyone who loves him will keep his word, and that the Father and he will \"make our home\" with that person. A short New Testament letter carries the name Jude, and its authorship is uncertain. People pray to Saint Jude for hard situations, which is a devotion and not a Church title.": 1,
      "Sources:": 1,
      "Benedict XVI: Simon and Jude, October 11, 2006": 1,
      ";": 1,
      "John 14": 1,
      ".": 1,
      "About Saint Jude Thaddeus": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "First century": 1
    },
    "magdalene-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Magdala on the Sea of Galilee; Jerusalem": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Disciple of Jesus; witness of the resurrection; \"apostle of the apostles\"": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "July 22": 1,
      "Mary takes her name from Magdala, a fishing town on the western shore of the Sea of Galilee. Luke 8 lists her among the women who traveled with Jesus and supported his ministry, and says seven demons had gone out of her. The Gospels place her near the cross and at the tomb.": 1,
      "John 20 tells how she stayed at the empty tomb in tears. She did not recognize Jesus until he said her name, and he sent her to tell the others. For this the Church calls her \"apostle of the apostles,\" and in 2016 the Holy See raised her July 22 memorial to a feast. The Gospels do not say she was a prostitute. That idea grew from confusing her with other women in the Gospels.": 1,
      "Sources:": 1,
      "Vatican News: St. Mary Magdalene, July 22": 1,
      ";": 2,
      "Holy See Press Office, June 10, 2016": 1,
      "John 20": 1,
      ".": 1,
      "About Saint Mary Magdalene": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "First century": 1
    },
    "marina-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Qalamoun hills and the Qadisha Valley, Lebanon": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Honored as a saint in Lebanese tradition": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "July 17 (Maronite calendar)": 1,
      "Lebanese tradition remembers Marina as a girl from the hills near Tripoli. Her father, Eugenius, wanted to become a monk after her mother died, and Marina asked not to be left behind. She cut her hair, took the name Marinos and entered the monastery of Qannoubine in the Qadisha Valley with him.": 1,
      "The tradition says she was falsely accused of fathering a child, accepted the blame in silence and raised the boy outside the monastery gate. Only after her death did the monks learn she was a woman. Her relics came to Lebanon on pilgrimage on July 17, 2018. The story is a tradition recorded in later sources, so this page gives no firm dates, and the Orthodox Church tells a different version.": 1,
      "Read the full account of Saint Marina": 1,
      "→": 1,
      "About Saint Marina of the Qadisha": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "Date uncertain; sources place her between the 5th and 8th centuries": 1
    },
    "maroun-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "A mountain near Cyrrhus, in present-day Syria": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Saint; father of the Maronite Church": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "February 9": 1,
      "Our main source for Maroun is Theodoret, Bishop of Cyrrhus, who wrote about him within a generation of his death. Maroun was a hermit who chose the top of a mountain and turned an old pagan temple into a church. He lived mostly in the open air, often prayed all night and fasted for weeks.": 1,
      "People traveled long distances to ask for his prayers. Theodoret says he healed both bodies and souls: greed, anger and sloth as well as illness. Saint John Chrysostom wrote to him from exile around the year 406. After his death his disciples built the monastery that became known as Bet Maroun, the \"House of Maron,\" and the Maronite Church takes its name from him. Saint Charbel was a monk of the Monastery of Saint Maron at Annaya.": 1,
      "Read the full life of Saint Maroun": 1,
      "→": 1,
      "Sources:": 1,
      "Eparchy of Saint Maron: Feast of Saint Maron, February 9": 1,
      ".": 1,
      "About Saint Maroun": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "Fourth to early fifth century": 1
    },
    "massabki-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Damascus, Syria": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Canonized October 20, 2024, by Pope Francis": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "July 10": 1,
      "Francis, Abdel Moati and Raphael Massabki were Maronite laymen of Damascus. Francis was a silk merchant and father of eight. Abdel Moati was a schoolteacher and father of five. Raphael, the youngest, was unmarried and helped in the family shop and the parish church.": 1,
      "On the night of July 9-10, 1860, violence against Christians reached Damascus. The brothers took shelter in the Franciscan convent of Saint Paul. They were offered their lives if they would give up their faith, and they refused. Eight Franciscan friars were killed with them. The Church honors all eleven as the Martyrs of Damascus. They were beatified in 1926 and canonized in 2024, the first Maronite laymen to be canonized.": 1,
      "Read the full account of the Massabki Brothers": 1,
      "→": 1,
      "Pope Francis: homily at the canonization Mass, October 20, 2024": 1,
      "About The Massabki Brothers": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "Martyred July 10, 1860": 1
    },
    "mother-teresa-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Skopje, then Calcutta, India": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Canonized September 4, 2016, by Pope Francis": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "September 5": 1,
      "Anjeze Gonxha Bojaxhiu was born on August 26, 1910 in Skopje, to an Albanian family. At 18 she joined the Loreto Sisters and went to India, where she taught for almost twenty years. On September 10, 1946, on a train to a retreat, she felt what she called \"a call within a call\" to serve the poorest of the poor.": 1,
      "In 1948 she began working in the slums of Calcutta. Her congregation, the Missionaries of Charity, was recognized in 1950. In 1952 she opened a home for the dying, and later orphanages and clinics followed. She received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1979 and died on September 5, 1997. Pope Francis canonized her in 2016.": 1,
      "Read the full life of Saint Teresa of Calcutta": 1,
      "→": 1,
      "Pope Francis: homily for the canonization of Mother Teresa": 1,
      "About Saint Teresa of Calcutta (Mother Teresa)": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "1910-1997": 1
    },
    "peter-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Bethsaida and Capernaum in Galilee; later Rome": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Apostle; first among the Twelve": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "June 29, with Saint Paul": 1,
      "Simon was a fisherman from Bethsaida, a town on the Sea of Galilee, who lived at Capernaum. He was married. Jesus gave him the name Cephas, in Greek Petros, \"rock.\" Peter is named about 154 times in the New Testament, more than anyone except Jesus.": 1,
      "The Gospels are honest about him: he trusted Jesus, then denied him on the night of his arrest, and was forgiven by the risen Jesus beside the lake. After Pentecost he led the first Christians, and Acts 12 tells of his rescue from prison. Ancient writers say he died in Rome. Beneath Saint Peter's Basilica lies the necropolis where tradition places his tomb.": 1,
      "About Saint Peter": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "First century": 1
    },
    "pio-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Pietrelcina and San Giovanni Rotondo, Italy": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Canonized June 16, 2002, by Pope John Paul II": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "September 23": 1,
      "Francesco Forgione was born on May 25, 1887 in Pietrelcina, a farming village in southern Italy. At fifteen he entered the Capuchin novitiate and took the name Pio. He was ordained a priest on August 10, 1910. His health was poor, and in 1916 he was sent to San Giovanni Rotondo, where he stayed for the rest of his life.": 1,
      "He heard confessions for many hours a day, and people came from all over Italy and beyond. He founded a hospital, the Casa Sollievo della Sofferenza, and prayer groups that now exist worldwide. He bore the stigmata from September 20, 1918 until his death on September 23, 1968, and his counsel is often quoted: \"Pray, hope, and don't worry.\"": 1,
      "Read the full life of Saint Padre Pio": 1,
      "→": 1,
      "Sources:": 1,
      "John Paul II: homily for the canonization of Padre Pio": 1,
      ".": 1,
      "About Saint Padre Pio": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "1887-1968": 1
    },
    "rafqa-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Himlaya, Aito and Jrabta, Lebanon": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Canonized June 10, 2001, by Pope John Paul II": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "March 23": 1,
      "Boutroussieh Choboq el-Rayess was born on June 29, 1832 in Himlaya, Lebanon. Her mother died when she was seven. After years of domestic service she joined a religious congregation, then in 1871 entered the Lebanese Maronite Order, the same order as Saint Charbel, and took the name Rafqa.": 1,
      "In 1885 she prayed to share in the suffering of Christ. Severe illness followed and she lost her sight, then most of the use of her body. Witnesses say she stayed patient and thankful, and she kept knitting with her hands. She died on March 23, 1914 at the monastery of Saint Joseph al-Dahr in Jrabta, where her shrine is today. She was the first woman of Lebanon to be canonized.": 1,
      "Read the full life of Saint Rafqa": 1,
      "→": 1,
      "Holy See: biography of Rafqa Choboq": 1,
      "About Saint Rafqa": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "1832-1914": 1
    },
