    "sergius-bacchus-story.html": {
      "Place": 1,
      "Roman Syria; their shrine city was Resafa": 1,
      "Status": 1,
      "Martyrs, honored in the East and West": 1,
      "Feast": 1,
      "October 7": 1,
      "Sergius and Bacchus were Roman army officers on the eastern frontier. The old account says the emperor valued them until they would not join in pagan worship. It says Bacchus died under a beating and Sergius was later beheaded. Their martyrdom is old and well attested. Many details come from a Greek account that historians treat partly as legend.": 1,
      "Their cult spread early. A shrine was built over Sergius's grave at Resafa in Syria by the end of the fourth century, and the town was later called Sergiopolis, \"city of Sergius.\" In Arabic they are Mar Sarkis and Bakhos. Their feast is October 7.": 1,
      "Sources:": 1,
      "Catholic Encyclopedia: Sergius and Bacchus": 1,
      ";": 1,
      "Livius.org: Resafa (Sergiopolis)": 1,
      ".": 1,
      "About Saints Sergius and Bacchus": 1,
      "Lived": 1,
      "Died around the year 303": 1
    },
